WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.240
SPEAKER: To give you a picture
of how amazing Spanner is,

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.174
let's start at the beginning,
just creating the database.

00:00:06.174 --> 00:00:07.590
Those of you who've
actually tried

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.170
to deploy a multi-region
database that's

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.050
highly scalable, highly
reliable and highly reliant

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:14.970
know that there's
like a zillion steps.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.530
You've got to
configure machines,

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:17.910
you've got to set
up replication,

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.280
you've got to worry about
failover, you've got sharding.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.399
What happens if I
have network issues?

00:00:22.399 --> 00:00:23.940
And then there's
the operational side

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.380
of just keeping
the thing running,

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.990
having to have full staffs of
people who do nothing but take

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:29.760
care of machinery.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.549
With Cloud Spanner, we
turn that into one machine.

00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:35.790
Robert's going to bring up
the dialogue to do that.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.800
He names the instance.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.510
He chooses whether
he wants it to be

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.150
a single or a
multi-regional database,

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.716
and then he says how many nodes.

00:00:43.716 --> 00:00:45.840
And in this case he picks
42, because that's always

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:46.548
the right answer.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:49.740
So that's it.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.500
We've now created a
multi-regional database

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.620
that has amazing capabilities
but that's getting started.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.250
Let's look at Spanner in
an actual real-world-scale

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.690
example.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.520
Imagine you're a ticket seller.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.110
You want to sell
tickets to events

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.880
worldwide to
customers worldwide.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.760
Now obviously it's
pretty important

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.550
that you sell a ticket
once and only once, right?

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.590
And it's also pretty
important that customers

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.170
get a good
performance experience

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.050
wherever in the world they
are buying their tickets.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.970
In this example, we've
deployed Cloud Spanner

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.800
into three Google regions--

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.420
US, Europe and Asia--

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.250
all connected by
that private network

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.320
that Urs told you about.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.750
We're also running workloads
that emulate customers

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.090
buying billions of tickets.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:35.040
In this case, we're
selling at about half

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.710
a million tickets per
minute through this system,

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:40.620
all through a system that is
maintaining consistent version

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.890
snapshots, full ACID
support for transactions,

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.050
full strong consistency.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:49.530
That's the power of Spanner as a
distributed base, a distributed

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:50.280
database.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.620
Our customers get a
low latency experience,

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:56.340
we get good old traditional
SQL consistency.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.700
Now, to prove that this really
is a database of some size,

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.090
let me show you the schema
behind this database.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.430
You can see it actually
is a SQL database.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.360
There's columns that
are joined together.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.389
And if you look at
that ticket data table,

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:12.090
it has three billion rows in it.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.250
So while we're
doing this, in fact,

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:17.500
we're running all this traffic
against a real SQL database.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:21.270
In fact, we can actually run SQL
queries against that database

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.150
while it's being hammered
with all that traffic.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.246
Here we have a query
and if you look

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:27.870
at the bottom part
of the WHERE clause,

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.960
you see that we're looking
for tickets in the US

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.530
that are still available
as of 10:00 p.m.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.030
and how many seats they have.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:39.881
So these are our top five
events with unsold tickets.

00:02:39.881 --> 00:02:41.880
Now what's important here
is what you don't see.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.270
What's remarkable is how
unremarkable this SQL looks.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.310
If you've deployed
a sharding system,

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:48.990
your SQL is a mess
because you're

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.150
trying to have to do joins
across multiple shards

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:51.910
of your database.

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:54.180
In fact, it's probably
not even human-writable

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:56.460
SQL when you use those systems.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:59.940
Spanner takes care of all that.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:01.770
But real-world
applications aren't static,

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.240
they change over time.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.251
What happens when
your needs change?

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:06.750
Well, first of all,
Spanner lets you

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:09.510
make transactionally
consistent schema updates

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.660
with no downtime, which is
critically important when

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.430
you're running systems that
your business depends on.

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.910
And what happens if you
get a wonderful success

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:19.290
and your needs grow?

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.520
Well, with the
traditional SQL database,

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:23.807
you better put a P.O. in
for a brand-new server.

00:03:23.807 --> 00:03:26.140
You better figure out how to
reconfigure the new server,

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.056
you better figure out
how to migrate the data,

00:03:28.056 --> 00:03:30.600
you better figure out
how to do cutover.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.630
Or if I'm doing scale
out, well, now I've

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.494
got to add a whole
new complex middleware

00:03:35.494 --> 00:03:37.410
layer that's incredibly
difficult to maintain.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.590
And even that takes time
and adds operational risk.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.710
In Cloud Spanner, Robert's
going to go back to the console

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:47.070
and show you how we add
capacity to a Spanner database.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:50.280
We simply go from 70
nodes to 99 nodes.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.560
That's it, we've scaled out.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.965
That's the power of a
horizontally scalable SQL

00:03:54.965 --> 00:03:55.465
database.

00:03:55.465 --> 00:03:58.712
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.990
And of course, since we use
Spanner so much internally,

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:07.940
we made sure it
handles large database.

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:10.490
Robert's going to switch over
to the monitoring console here,

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:13.250
and what I want you to look
at is that graph on the bottom

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:13.934
left.

00:04:13.934 --> 00:04:15.350
We've been running
this whole demo

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:18.826
against an 80-terabyte database.

00:04:18.826 --> 00:04:20.450
And if you look at
the top right graph,

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:23.090
when we loaded the data
in, we loaded the data

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:26.510
at an average of 800
megabytes per second.

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:30.830
And we peaked at about a gig
per second of loading data.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.770
That's amazing,
that's crazy fast.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:35.270
So that's Cloud Spanner.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:38.810
You create a highly reliable
performant, fully managed

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.490
multi-region
database in seconds.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.100
Cloud Spanner is SQL.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:45.980
You get interoperability in
the power of SQL queries.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:48.350
You get ACID transactions.

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:52.160
Plus, you get advanced features
like on the fly schema changes.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:53.665
Cloud Spanner's fully managed.

00:04:53.665 --> 00:04:55.790
We never had to worry about
setting up replication,

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:57.290
sharding, failover.

00:04:57.290 --> 00:05:00.600
The system actually even tunes
itself automatically over time.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.290
So as you use it, it
learns your query patterns

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:07.040
and it optimizes its own
performance characteristics.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.870
And Cloud Spanner
is built for scale.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:11.240
We've been running our
largest production systems

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:12.891
on this for years.

00:05:12.891 --> 00:05:14.390
So with that, I'll
turn it back over

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:16.223
to Urs for a little bit
more of the keynote.

00:05:16.223 --> 00:05:17.124
Thanks.

